<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Aegates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/241-bce">241 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Battle of the Aegates Islands · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Aegates was a naval battle fought in 241 BCE off the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, in which the Roman fleet defeated the Carthaginian navy. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> was forced to surrender Sicily and pay a heavy indemnity, a serious setback in the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> began in 264 BCE and by 248 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> held only Lilybaeum and Drepana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, both on the west coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had won the earlier large naval battles with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvus">corvus</a>, a boarding device, but lost fleets to storms. Carthage then let its navy run down. By 243 BCE both states were exhausted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was almost bankrupt. The Senate asked its wealthiest citizens for loans to build one ship each, repayable from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and a fleet of about 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a> was built, modelled on a captured Carthaginian vessel. The corvus was abandoned, so that the Romans would have to be better sailors rather than better soldiers.</p><p>Gaius Lutatius Catulus and Quintus Valerius Falto led it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 242 BCE and blockaded both cities, drilling their crews. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> took nine months to ready 250 warships and many transports, but could not train the crews properly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Carthaginian fleet, under a commander named Hanno, gathered off Hiera in early March 241 BCE, planning to reach Lilybaeum on a following wind, unload grain and embark soldiers as marines. Roman scouts saw it. Catulus took soldiers from the besieging army as marines and anchored off Aegusa. On 10 March a strong west wind blew, and Hanno set sail. Catulus decided to attack against the wind and had his ships stripped of masts and sails. Injured, he left command to Falto.</p><p>The fleets met west of Phorbantia. The Carthaginian ships were laden with supplies, short of marines and crewed by hurriedly levied men, while the Roman ships were light and better handled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sank 50 Carthaginian ships and captured 70 with 10,000 men, losing 30 ships sunk and 50 damaged. A change of wind let the rest flee, as the Romans had no sails to pursue.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hanno was crucified at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Catulus and Falto each received a triumph, and Catulus built a temple to Juturna in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Campus Martius</a>. The Carthaginian Senate was reluctant to build another fleet, and Carthage sued for peace. By the Treaty of Lutatius it surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and paid substantial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, and Rome became the leading military power of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Aegates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Punic War of 264–241 BCE, which began over conflicting interests in Sicily, lasted twenty-three years and ended with Carthage’s naval defeat at the <b>Battle of the Aegates Islands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Pyrrhus of Epirus won costly victories at Heraclea in 280 BCE and Asculum in 279 BCE but withdrew after Beneventum in 275 BCE; in the First Punic War of 264–241 BCE Rome built fleets and forced Carthage out of Sicily after the <b>Aegates Islands</b> in 241 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li></ul></section>
